    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Recent studies have established that the Ribosomal S6 Kinases (RSKs) drive cell growth, survival, and proliferation in solid cancers including breast, lung, and prostate. However, the role of RSK in normal and malignant blood cell formation, or hematopoiesis, remains undetermined. RSK phosphorylation targets such as the transcription factor cyclic AMP response element binding protein (CREB) are known to be regulators of hematopoietic differentiation as well as potential therapeutic targets for acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Here, we describe the function of RSKs in hematopoietic differentiation and growth. RSK1 and RSK2 were identified as the predominant isoforms in hematopoietic cells, particularly in the myeloid lineage. RSK inhibition using the small molecule inhibitor BI-D1870 selectively reduced AML cell survival and development as compared to normal hematopoietic cells. When utilized to investigate the isoform-specific function of RSKs in hematopoiesis, small hairpin RNA (shRNA) knockdown of either RSK1 or RSK2 in AML cells failed to induce a growth defect. Additionally, knockdown of RSK1 and RSK2 together slightly weakened CREB activation but also did not impair cell growth, indicating that residual expression and redundant function of RSKs in knockdown cells allowed for relatively normal growth of RSK knockdown AML cells. A large reduction in AML cell growth was only observed when RSK1 or RSK2 were completely knocked out using CRISPR. Taken together, our data demonstrate that RSK is crucial for the tumorigenesis and proliferation of AML cells as well as normal myeloid development (myelopoiesis). Increased susceptibility of AML cells to RSK inhibition signify RSK’s potential as a future therapeutic target for AML.",
